{"entries":[{"h":"Habergeon","m":"a small coat of mail covering the head and shoulders","n":"Wright says the hauberk and habergeon are distinct terms in old writers.","refs":["2:28:32","2:39:23","14:26:14","16:4:16","18:41:26"]},{"h":"Had","m":"held","n":"A usage like the Latin once common.","pos":"pp.","refs":["44:25:26"]},{"h":"Haft","m":"handle","n":"From Old English: that by which anything is held.","pos":"sb.","refs":["7:3:22"]},{"h":"Hale","m":"to pull with force","n":"From French; now common as \"haul\".","pos":"v.t.","refs":["42:12:58","44:8:3"]},{"h":"Halt","m":"to limp, walk lamely","pos":"v.i.","refs":["1:32:31","19:38:17"]},{"h":"Hand","m":"to combine","pos":"v.i.","refs":[]},{"h":"Hand","m":"side","pos":"sb.","refs":[]},{"h":"Handbreadth","m":"a palm, a measure of length","n":"A measure now rarely used; horses are still measured by hands.","pos":"sb.","refs":["19:39:5"]},{"h":"Handle","m":"to treat","n":"From Old English; like Latin, the sense is metaphorical.","pos":"v.t.","refs":["20:16:20","47:4:2"]},{"etc":true,"h":"Handmaid","m":"a female servant","refs":["1:16:1"]},{"h":"Handstaves","m":"weapons of some kind","n":"The margin gives \"javelins\", but the word is a literal rendering of the Hebrew.","pos":"sb.","refs":[]},{"h":"Handywork","m":"workmanship","pos":"sb.","refs":["19:19:1"]},{"h":"Haply","m":"perchance, perhaps","pos":"adv.","refs":["9:14:30","41:11:13"]},{"h":"Hard","m":"close, near","pos":"adv.","refs":["7:9:52","19:63:8","44:18:7"]},{"h":"Hardly","m":"with difficulty","n":"This is its literal meaning.","pos":"adv.","refs":["40:19:23"]},{"h":"Hardness","m":"hardship","pos":"sb.","refs":["55:2:3"]},{"h":"Harnessed","m":"armed","n":"The margin has \"five in a rank\", owing to doubt about the Hebrew root; Wright says the text meaning is still preferred.","pos":"pp.","refs":["2:13:18"]},{"h":"Harp","m":"to play upon the harp","pos":"v.t.","refs":["46:14:7","66:14:2"]},{"h":"Harp","m":"to play upon the harp","pos":"v.i.","refs":["46:14:7","6